# The fourth position a typeset's name can stand in, and the one both engines
# agreed on while both were wrong. A constructor pattern destructures a record
# by its type; a typeset has no fields to destructure and no value is ever
# one, so the arm can never match whatever the caller passes. What it said
# instead was "no overload of `area` matches these arguments", at run time,
# which sends the reader to look at an argument that is fine.
#
# The caret sits on the first field rather than on `shape`, because
# `Pattern::Ctor` carries no span. Giving it one was built and measured:
# `Pattern` grows from 64 bytes to 72, and `kanso check lib/json` pays 482,913
# more instructions for the exact column.
type circle
  r

type square
  s

type shape circle square

fn area (shape x)
  x

pub play = print "{area (circle 1)}"
